{"detail": {"id": "ds4a-ac6", "book": "DS4A", "chapter": 8, "exercise_number": "AC 6", "title": "Interpretability versus Complexity", "category": "Decision trees", "source_page": "p. 400", "kind": "training", "required_dataset": "penguins", "origin": "textbook", "description": "Create three decision trees for predicting species in the penguins data with max depth 1, 5 and 7. Plot them and answer: (1) which variable is at the top of all trees? (2) Based on the bottom nodes, which tree appears most accurate? (3) Rank the trees by complexity and interpretability (fill in the blanks). (4) STAR.", "concepts": ["max depth", "complexity", "interpretability", "nested trees", "trade-off"], "understand": "DS4A \u00a78.3: max depth controls how complex a tree may become. More splits capture more structure (complexity \u2191) but each prediction needs more questions (interpretability \u2193). Node counts are in alphabetical order: Adelie, Chinstrap, Gentoo.", "questions": [{"id": "1", "prompt": "Which variable appears at the top of all of these trees?", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}, {"id": "2", "prompt": "Based on the nodes at the very bottom of each tree, which tree appears to be the most accurate?", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}, {"id": "3", "prompt": "Rank the trees in order of complexity and interpretability (fill in the blanks).", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}, {"id": "4", "prompt": "Describe the results using the STAR Framework.", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}], "interpretation": "With scikit-learn the depth-5 tree already classifies every training penguin correctly, so allowing depth 7 changes nothing. 'Most accurate' here means on training data only.", "common_mistakes": ["Concluding that 100% training accuracy means the best model (it may be overfitted).", "Expecting the depth-7 tree to have depth 7: growth stops when leaves are pure.", "Comparing with the book's R plots: R's rpart has extra stopping rules (minsplit = 20, cp = 0.01 by default), so its deep trees are smaller."], "python_code": "import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n\npenguins = pd.read_csv(\"penguins.csv\")\nX, y = penguins.drop(columns=\"species\"), penguins[\"species\"]\nencode = ColumnTransformer([(\"dummify\", OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False), [\"island\", \"sex\"])],\n                           remainder=\"passthrough\")\nfor depth in (1, 5, 7):\n    model = Pipeline([(\"prep\", encode),\n                      (\"tree\", DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=depth, random_state=1234))]).fit(X, y)\n    t = model[\"tree\"]\n    print(depth, \"leaves:\", t.get_n_leaves(), \"actual depth:\", t.get_depth(),\n          \"training accuracy:\", round(model.score(X, y), 4))\n", "parameters": {"depths": [1, 5, 7], "random_state": 1234}, "notes": ["The book's own Python script for the in-chapter TiY uses one-hot encoding for island and sex and passes the other columns through; this app does the same."]}, "solve": {"answers": [{"id": "1", "answer": "flipper_length_mm", "explanation": "All trees start with the same root split because trees are grown greedily: the best first question does not depend on how deep the tree is allowed to grow (DS4A Quick Tip: the trees are nested)."}, {"id": "2", "answer": "max depth 5 (tied with the deeper tree)", "explanation": "Leaf correct counts: depth 1: 262/333 = 0.787; depth 5: 333/333 = 1.000; depth 7: 333/333 = 1.000. Note: the max_depth 7 tree is IDENTICAL to the max_depth 5 tree \u2014 at depth 5 every leaf is already pure (one species), so there is nothing left to split. max_depth is an upper limit, not a target."}, {"id": "3", "answer": {"most_interpretable_least_complex": "first", "because_first": "a single split", "least_interpretable_most_complex": "third", "because_third": "the most splits", "variables": "more", "characterize": "more challenging", "middle": "second", "complexity_rank": ["depth 1 (1 splits)", "depth 5 (12 splits)", "depth 7 (12 splits)"]}, "explanation": "The first tree asks one question (1 split, features ['flipper_length_mm']); the third has 12 splits using ['bill_depth_mm', 'bill_length_mm', 'flipper_length_mm', 'island_Dream', 'sex_female']. Note: the max_depth 7 tree is IDENTICAL to the max_depth 5 tree \u2014 at depth 5 every leaf is already pure (one species), so there is nothing left to split. max_depth is an upper limit, not a target."}, {"id": "4", "answer": {"Scope": "333 penguins with complete records; explanatory variables island, bill length/depth, flipper length, body mass, sex, year; response species (Adelie, Chinstrap, Gentoo). Source: DS4A dataset (palmerpenguins).", "Track": "How does allowing a deeper (more complex) tree change interpretability and accuracy when predicting species?", "Articulate": "Depth 1 uses only flipper_length_mm and is correct for 262/333 penguins; it can never predict Chinstrap. Deeper trees use more variables and reach 333/333 correct on the training data, but are much harder to describe. Note: the max_depth 7 tree is IDENTICAL to the max_depth 5 tree \u2014 at depth 5 every leaf is already pure (one species), so there is nothing left to split. max_depth is an upper limit, not a target.", "Respond": "Training accuracy of 100% does not prove the deep tree generalizes: split into training and test data and compare test accuracy (see the Overfitting lab). Prefer the simplest tree with good test performance."}, "explanation": "STAR framework."}], "trees": [{"max_depth": 1, "tree": {"task": "classification", "criterion": "gini", "classes": ["Adelie", "Chinstrap", "Gentoo"], "n_nodes": 3, "n_leaves": 2, "n_splits": 1, "depth": 1, "features_used": ["flipper_length_mm"], "feature_importances": {"flipper_length_mm": 1.0}, "nodes": [{"id": 0, "depth": 0, "parent": -1, "is_leaf": false, "samples": 333, "impurity": 0.6383680978275572, "left": 1, "right": 2, 